  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nCurrent water vapor imagery and RAP analysis depict a mid/upper \nridge building over the Four Corners as the first of a series of \nupper impulses sits near the Pacific Northwest. At the surface, a \ncold front spans from the Red River Valley to the Great Lakes as \nhigh pressure resides over the Central Plains. Gradual \ndestabilization through the remainder of this afternoon and evening \nmay allow for an isolated shower/storm or two to develop along and \nwest of US-281, with better (albeit low) chances residing to the \nwest of the forecast area. Solid DCAPE values indicate the potential \nfor stout wind gusts with the strongest activity during this \ntimeframe. Later tonight into Friday morning, a mid-level baroclinic \nzone setting up over the area may provide additional low chances for \nisolated showers and storms especially across central and northeast \nKansas. A few isolated strong wind gusts may continue to be possible \nwith the strongest activity, though severe storms appear unlikely.\n\nFollowing one more day of seasonably warm highs in the low to middle \n90s on Friday, the mid/upper ridge will continue to build east into \nthe weekend. This will promote the return of afternoon highs near \n100 degrees alongside mostly dry conditions. A slight change in the \npattern may look to arrive Sunday and last into the beginning of the \nwork week as a handful of mid-level perturbations track out of the \nRockies and into the Northern Plains. These will help slowly drive a \nfrontal boundary southward, with model consensus supporting a \nplacement Sunday evening from northwest Kansas to the northeast into \nsouthern Minnesota. On-and-off shower and storm chances may appear \nalong this boundary throughout the week as it stalls, which means \nthe best opportunities for rain should reside across portions of \ncentral Kansas to the west and north. The placement for these \nchances may change as additional model runs become available, though \nas it stands with this forecast cycle, most of the area looks to \nremain dry.\n\nSouth of the aforementioned stalled front, high temperatures in the \n100s are progged for many locations for a majority of next week as \nthe mid/upper ridge shifts toward the southeast CONUS. Despite this \nheat, breezy south winds and dewpoints in the middle to upper 50s \nshould keep conditions from feeling quite as hot, especially for the \nfirst half of the week. Additionally, if the front is able to \nwork its way southward into the forecast area, slightly cooler \ntemperatures in the trailing airmass should be realized\nespecially toward the latter half of the week. Stay tuned as \nadditional details become available.",
